# Bolna WhatsApp Reply Loop: configuration reference. NAMES ONLY, no values.
#
# This project runs inside n8n, so there is no .env file to load. This file lists
# what you must create in your own n8n instance and services. It is derived from
# the variables and credentials the exported workflows actually reference.
# Never commit real values.

# --- n8n Variables (Settings > Variables; read in nodes as $vars.NAME) ---------
# Exact OpenAI model ID. Copy it from the OpenAI models page; it must support
# Structured Outputs. Do not use "latest"-style aliases.
BOLNA_LLM_MODEL=
# Public production URL of the WF2 webhook, exactly as Twilio calls it
# (used to validate X-Twilio-Signature).
BOLNA_TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Twilio auth token of the account that owns the WhatsApp sandbox. The only
# secret held in a variable, because the signature check runs in a Code node.
BOLNA_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# WhatsApp sandbox sender, E.164, e.g. the number shown in the Twilio console.
TWILIO_SANDBOX_NUMBER=

# --- n8n Credentials (names as used by the nodes in workflows/) ---------------
# "Bolna - Supabase"        Supabase API credential: project URL + service_role key
# "Bolna - Twilio Sandbox"  Twilio API credential: Account SID + auth token
# "Bolna - OpenAI"          Header Auth: header "Authorization", value "Bearer <your key>"
# "Slack - Alerts Bot"      Slack API credential (bot token with permission to post)

# --- Placeholders inside the exported workflow JSON ----------------------------
# <YOUR_SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF>   your Supabase project ref (in the REST URLs)
# <YOUR_SLACK_CHANNEL_ID>       ID of the channel that receives alerts

# --- Outside n8n ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Bolna: set the agent webhook URL to the WF1 production URL
#        (https://<your-n8n-host>/webhook/bolna-call-intake).
# Twilio: set the sandbox "When a message comes in" URL to the WF2 production URL
#        (https://<your-n8n-host>/webhook/bolna-twilio-reply), method POST.
# A Bolna API key is only needed if you place test calls yourself; the workflows
# do not use it. Keep it out of the repo and out of synced tools.
